{:edb/db-before-t 2204 :edb/db-after-t 2205 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535517 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:39:21.525Z" 13194139535517 true] [17592186166434 1100 "Suppose $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = 6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = -3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n3. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = (1+t^2)\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + e^{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$" 13194139535517 false] [17592186166434 1100 "Suppose $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = 6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = -3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = (1+t^2)\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + e^{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$" 13194139535517 true] [17592186166434 1145 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2$, the linear combination\n$c_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$\nis also a solution of the system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n$6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1$, respectively.\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n$-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4$, respectively.\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$. Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535517 false] [17592186166434 1145 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ the linear combination \n\n$$\nc_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535517 true] [17592186166435 1100 "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n2. $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n3. $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" 13194139535517 false] [17592186166435 1100 "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" 13194139535517 true] [17592186166435 1145 "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nthen $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$, $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthen any linear combination\n$c_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus,\n$\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus,\n$\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535517 false] [17592186166435 1145 "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),$ then $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t),$ $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535517 true] [17592186166436 1100 "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/32e9cba3d7ee75019489dcc8a9c82f1b854bfa77af49e22fa9f51d4412cba95d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/dc64b0c557c838cf31e652ebdb8eee91d1fee8c947b343b16fceaf765c1f8517.png) are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/64b157c381c05102b51320592e61ffff5c4d611af4bd15679f1f2b38f3d01b10.png)" 13194139535517 true] [17592186166436 1100 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{4t} \\\\ 6e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535517 false] [17592186166436 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthe superposition principle guarantees that $c\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is also a solution to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nSo, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAw &= w^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(4e^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6e^{5t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16e^{4t} \\\\ 30e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535517 false] [17592186166436 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\nc\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).$ So, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6{e}^{5t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16{e}^{4t} \\\\ 30{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535517 true] [17592186166831 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353249 13194139535517 true] [17592186166831 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353284 13194139535517 true] [17592186166831 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353291 13194139535517 true] [17592186166831 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353298 13194139535517 true] [17592186166832 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353305 13194139535517 true] [17592186166832 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353312 13194139535517 true] [17592186166832 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353319 13194139535517 true] [17592186166832 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353326 13194139535517 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353256 13194139535517 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353263 13194139535517 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353270 13194139535517 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353277 13194139535517 true] [17592186167077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186163880 13194139535517 false] [17592186167077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353243 13194139535517 true] [17592186167077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535517 true] [17592186167077 1100 "If\n\n$u(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$v(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ -\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$\nare solutions to $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t - \\cos t \\\\ \\cos t + \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535517 false] [17592186167077 1100 "If\n$u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535517 true] [17592186167077 1145 "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535517 true] [17592186167116 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535517 true] [17592186167116 1100 "Let $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$ and $x_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n1. $3 \\cdot x_{1}(t) + x_{p}(t)$\n2. $x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t) + 2 \\cdot x_{p}(t)$\n3. $-4 \\cdot x_{2}(t) + x_{p}(t)$" 13194139535517 false] [17592186167116 1100 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" 13194139535517 true] [17592186167116 1145 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535517 true] [17592186167118 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535517 true] [17592186167118 1100 "Suppose $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n1. $x(t) = e^{t}x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$\n2. $x(t) = x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$\n3. $x(t) = x_{1}(t) \\cdot x_{2}(t)$ (cross-product)" 13194139535517 false] [17592186167118 1100 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={e}^{t}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$ (cross-product)" 13194139535517 true] [17592186167118 1145 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the first term is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353244 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353245 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353246 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353247 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353248 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1041 "selection" 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353246 13194139535517 true] [17592186353243 1110 #uuid "962da458-6f6b-5aba-89de-48364475a6f1" 13194139535517 true] [17592186353244 1034 "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535517 true] [17592186353244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535517 true] [17592186353244 1111 #uuid "