298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535517 true] [17592186353298 1100 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" 13194139535517 true] [17592186353298 1113 #uuid "ca8f9356-4beb-53da-a0cd-65d91466d466" 13194139535517 true] [17592186353298 1145 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353300 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353301 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353302 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353303 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353304 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535517 true] [17592186353299 1041 "selection" 13194139535517